Move available-services cache out of Settings into its own table (v0.140)

Dan's report after v0.139: the DPD sweep now runs with no errors (fixed in
v0.139), and APC's only warning is the expected "Ireland - no services" one,
but the Available Services list still wasn't growing. He asked to replace
the Settings-based cache with a courier-specific data table instead of
fixing the symptom again.

Root cause: DpdShippingService/ApcShippingService.cacheLastKnownServices()
serialized the whole known-services list as JSON into a single Settings row
(dpd_last_known_services/apc_last_known_services). That row's column
(app_settings.setting_value) was VARCHAR(500) (V23 migration) - plenty for
the handful of short config strings Settings originally held, but nowhere
near enough once a sweep across 9 postcodes accumulated DPD/APC's often
fairly verbose service descriptions. Postgres's "value too long for type
character varying(500)" exception on save was being silently swallowed by
a try/catch that only logged a server-side warning - so the sweep's own
HTTP calls could all succeed (no postcode-level warnings at all) while the
save that was supposed to remember the results quietly failed every single
time, with nothing surfaced to the warnings list or the frontend.

Fix - a real table instead of a capped blob:
- New `courier_service_options` table (courier, code, label, extra_label,
  enabled - one row per courier+code, TEXT columns so there's no practical
  size ceiling to hit again) and entity/repository
  (CourierServiceOption/CourierServiceOptionRepository).
- DpdShippingService/ApcShippingService's cacheLastKnownServices(),
  loadLastKnownServices(), disabledServiceCodes(), setDisabledServices()
  and listAllKnownServicesForToggle() all rewritten against the new table -
  no more JSON serialization, and a code's enabled flag is a real column
  that can be flipped without rewriting the whole list.
- Migration (V55) also widens app_settings.setting_value from VARCHAR(500)
  to TEXT, so no other setting can hit this same silent ceiling in future,
  and does a best-effort carry-over of whatever's currently cached (skipped
  per-courier, not failed outright, if that cached value is itself
  malformed/truncated from the original bug).

